Kali Linux and Ubuntu Download & Installation Guide (VMware Fusion)
This page covers downloading and installing Kali Linux and Ubuntu as virtual machines in VMware Fusion on macOS. It builds on the base VMware Fusion installation already documented.

Critical First Step: Check Your Mac's Architecture
Before downloading anything, confirm whether your Mac uses Apple Silicon (M1/M2/M3/M4/M5) or an Intel processor. This determines which ISO image you need:

Apple Silicon Macs require ARM64 (aarch64) images. x86_64 ISOs will not boot.

Intel Macs require x86_64 (amd64) images.

You can check by going to Apple Menu → About This Mac.

Part 1: Kali Linux
Download Kali Linux
Go to the official Kali download page: https://www.kali.org/get-kali/.

Select Installer Images (recommended over Live or NetInstaller for a full offline installation).

For Apple Silicon: choose the Apple Silicon (ARM64) installer image.

For Intel: choose the standard 64-bit (amd64) installer image.

Save the .iso file and note its location.

Security Note: Kali strongly recommends verifying your ISO checksum before use. Download the SHA256SUMS file alongside the ISO and compare hashes using shasum -a 256 <filename>.iso in Terminal.

Create the Kali VM in VMware Fusion
Open VMware Fusion. From the menu bar, select File → New.

Choose Install from disc or image and browse to your downloaded Kali ISO.

VMware Fusion should detect the OS as Debian 12.x 64-bit Arm (Apple Silicon) or Debian 12.x 64-bit (Intel). Click Continue.

Click Customize Settings to adjust resources before finishing:

RAM: At least 4 GB (4096 MB) recommended for Kali with a desktop environment.

CPU: 2 or more cores.

Disk: 20–40 GB is typical, depending on your use case.

Save the VM and click Finish.

Apple Silicon M4+ Note: Some users on M4 and newer Macs have reported the VMware Fusion GUI wizard freezing with the "Continue" button greyed out when creating a Kali VM. If this happens, the VM can be created manually via Terminal using a .vmx configuration file. This workaround is documented but outside the scope of this basic guide.

Install Kali Linux
Start the VM. The Kali installer boot menu appears. Select Graphical install.

Follow the prompts:

Language: Select your preferred language.

Location: Select your country/timezone.

Keyboard: Choose your layout.

Hostname: A name for the VM (e.g., kali).

User account: Set a username and password.

Disk partitioning: For a simple VM, choose Guided - use entire disk and confirm.

When prompted for software selection, the default selections (Xfce desktop, top10 tools) are appropriate for most users.

Complete the installation and reboot the VM when prompted.

Part 2: Ubuntu
Download Ubuntu
Go to the official Ubuntu Desktop download page: https://ubuntu.com/download/desktop.

Choose the architecture that matches your Mac:

Apple Silicon: Click ARM 64-bit architecture and download the ISO.

Intel: Download the standard Intel or AMD 64-bit ISO.

The current LTS release is recommended for stability .
Save the .iso file.

Verification: Ubuntu also provides SHA256 checksums. It is good practice to verify your download before installing.

Create the Ubuntu VM in VMware Fusion
Open VMware Fusion and select File → New.

Choose Install from disc or image and select your Ubuntu ISO.

VMware Fusion may detect the OS as Ubuntu 64-bit (or Ubuntu 64-bit Arm on Apple Silicon). If not, select Other Linux 6.x kernel 64-bit as a fallback.

Click Customize Settings:

RAM: 4 GB minimum recommended (8 GB if your Mac has enough memory).

CPU: 2–4 cores.

Disk: 50 GB(as I am running my wazuh manager in ubuntu)

Save and finish.

Install Ubuntu
Start the VM. The GRUB menu appears; select Try or Install Ubuntu.

Choose your language and keyboard layout.

Network: Select Use wired connection .

Installation type: Choose Interactive installation.

Applications: The Default selection provides essentials (browser, basic utilities). Choose Extended selection if you want office tools.

Disk setup: Select Erase disk and install Ubuntu (this only affects the VM's virtual disk, not your Mac's real disk).

User account: Enter your name, a computer name (hostname), username, and password.

Wait for installation to complete (typically 15–30 minutes) and restart the VM when prompted.
